WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Good to have you on the DP Pod, the due positive podcast episode 15.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.400
I'm Dan Pere, joined by Patrick Novak, it's July 19th, 2026.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.300
It's exactly what it sounds like.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.420
The good news, the honest numbers, and something to do about it.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.580
Morning Dan, I spent last night rereading the QLED paper

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.140
and I still can't stop grinning at how clean the inverse design step turned out.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.760
Inverse-design.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.760
Chemist word for the model to the homework we used to do by hand for years.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:29.500
Exactly.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.020
And the result is not a marginal bump.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.400
It is a full doubling of efficiency plus a 40-fold lifetime gain.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.360
40-fold.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.120
That's the hook that got me to sit up.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.160
Let's get into it.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.840
Time for the positive papers.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.880
Lead with the QLED story.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.100
Researchers trained an AI on existing data sets instead of running hundreds of physical samples.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.800
The model then proposed complete process recipes that cut decades of trial and error.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.560
The mechanism is straightforward once you see it.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.360
Temperature, precursor ratios, and deposition rates interact

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.240
in ways that are hard to map with one variable at a time experiments.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:10.520
The model learns the hidden relationships and hands back a full recipe that can be tested in one run.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.860
One run instead of a year of lab time.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.620
That survives contact with real manufacturing schedules.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.480
The numbers are still early, but the test conditions already show twice the efficiency

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.440
and roughly 40 times the lifetime.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.360
Everyday screens could draw less power and last longer before replacement.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.080
I'm buying the efficiency win.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.840
The lifetime claim makes me pause.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.040
Accelerated aging tests are useful, but panels in the field face humidity cycles,

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:38.840
voltage spikes, and temperature swings we don't fully replicate in the lab.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:39.720
Fair push.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.500
The paper itself flags the need for commercial scale validation.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.040
Still, the approach itself is the bigger story.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.120
It replaces months or years of sequential mixing and measuring

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.020
with a single modeled proposal.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.260
So the real lever is speed to iteration.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.120
One good recipe now frees the team to test the next variable faster.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.820
Next story, the Shixilube impactor that ended the dinosaurs.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.400
Isotopic mapping shows it was a rare CO-condrite from the outer solar system,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.780
not a common near-earth asteroid.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.140
That detail changes the extinction model.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.820
The chemistry points to planet cooling dust clouds as the main driver

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.880
rather than sulfur from the impactor itself.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.340
Dust stays aloft longer than sulfur aerosols,

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.280
which matches the simultaneous collapse of plant and plankton records.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:32.320
The same prolonged darkness explains why marine and terrestrial ecosystems failed together.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:35.960
One well-studied crater still leaves the generalization question open.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.440
We don't know yet how many other extinctions followed the same pattern.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:40.040
True.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:44.920
The data set is limited, but it sharpens how we model rare solar system objects

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.980
that can still reshape climate on human time scales.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.480
Third paper, an asteroid breakup 800 million years ago

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.020
in your Jupiter's gravitational gateway sent fragments

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.980
Earthward in a concentrated pulse.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.940
Lunar samples and impact melt ages cluster around that date,

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.300
and Mars shows a matching crater spike.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.440
On Earth, the timing lines up with the cryogenian glaciations

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.600
and the later rise of complex multicellular life.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.280
The model stops short of claiming the impacts cause the glaciations.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.260
It only says they added external forcing while internal climate feedbacks

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.080
were already active.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.120
Still useful, it gives us a concrete mechanism

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.840
for how a single breakup event can deliver a short,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.780
intense bombardment instead of a steady rain over millions of years.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.840
If I were scheduling observation time,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.480
I'd want tighter crater dating on the moon and Mars

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.920
before I adjust any Earth climate priors.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:35.640
Reasonable.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.160
More data will tighten the size, distribution, and timing.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.560
Time to think positive.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:44.800
Carol Dweck's growth mindset work shows that treating abilities as

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.900
developable rather than fixed changes how people respond to setbacks.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.500
The QLED result is a live example.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:57.620
Years of stalled progress gave way once better tools replaced brute force trials.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.780
Same lens fits the asteroid papers.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.220
Decades of incremental sample collection and isotopic work

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.960
eventually produced a clearer picture.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.040
The mental rep today is simple.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.540
Pick one task you have avoided because it feels fixed and difficulty.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.420
Then list the smallest next experiment you could run before the day ends.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.080
That single listed step turns an open-ended worry into a bounded test

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:20.640
whose result can be measured.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:22.300
That brings us to the lever.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.640
Join a local citizen science project collecting data on river fish runs

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.300
or night sky observations.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.640
Most programs are free and ask for one to two hours per month

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.440
of counting or photo uploads through a phone app.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.100
The time cost stays low.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.720
The data scales with participation because observatories and agencies

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.720
already run the verification pipelines.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.920
Night sky programs especially benefit from wide geographic coverage since meteors appear

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.600
unpredictable.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.860
Fish run counts help managers adjust harvest limits in real time.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.140
Both data types feed the same impact models.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.840
The 800 million year study is refining.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.020
The Honest caveat is the usual one.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.060
Start with established university or government portals that publish data openly.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.460
They also supply training sets so new volunteers can check their own submissions.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:13.760
If even 1% of regular listeners added two hours monthly,

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.980
the aggregate would grow by thousands of verified observations per year.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:19.940
Now the do positive dispatch.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.620
Try the citizen science project on night sky observations.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.540
Tell us what you logged and whether the two-hour monthly commitment felt manageable.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.060
I'm doing this one this week.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:31.080
I'll report back.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.140
Send your counts or photos to the show and we'll read them here.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:37.180
And if you like the solar-system angle today,

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:40.120
fascinating frontiers just covered NASA's

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:45.280
psyche spacecraft using Mars as a gravitational slingshot and the data set it sent back.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.240
That's it for today.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.720
Positive vibes, positive science, and one honest number to act on.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.000
Thanks for spending 10 minutes on the good news.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.480
I'm Patrick Novak.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:54.480
I'm Dan Perra.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:55.540
Do something about it.

